## The idea

A tree on n nodes has **exactly n − 1 edges**. With that count, "no cycle" already guarantees "connected". So: check the count, then add edges with union-find — an edge whose ends are already joined would close a cycle.

## Step by step

1. If edges ≠ n − 1 → false.
2. Union-find: every node its own group.
3. For each edge: if both ends have the same root → cycle → false. Else join.
4. Return true.

## Worked example

**Input:** n = 5, edges 0-1, 1-2, 2-3, 1-3, 1-4

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| count | 5 edges ≠ 4 → false immediately |

**Result:** false (it has a cycle 1-2-3)

## Cost

O(n · α(n)) — practically linear.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 261 — do these undirected edges on nodes 0..n-1 form exactly one tree?
public static class ValidTree {
    public static bool Check(int n, int[][] edges) {
        edges ??= new int[0][];
        // A tree on n nodes has exactly n - 1 edges. With that many, "no cycle" also means "connected".
        if (edges.Length != n - 1) return false;
        var parent = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
        foreach (var e in edges) {
            int a = Root(parent, e[0]), b = Root(parent, e[1]);
            if (a == b) return false;          // already connected: this edge closes a cycle
            parent[a] = b;                     // join the two groups
        }
        return true;
    }

    // Union-find root with path halving: each lookup shortens the path for the next one.
    private static int Root(int[] parent, int x) {
        while (parent[x] != x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
        return x;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why is the edge count enough with union-find?**

n − 1 edges and no cycle means n − (n − 1) = 1 component.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n α(n)) — effectively linear.

**Q: BFS instead?**

Also fine: count edges, then check one BFS reaches all n nodes.

**Q: Edges arrive from many threads.**

Union-find under one lock is simple; lock-free union-find exists but is rarely worth it.

**Q: A billion nodes.**

Distributed connected components (label propagation) — far harder; say so.

**Q: Related?**

InterviewDB Q15 (task dependencies) is the directed version: cycle detection by topological sort.

